[[{:db/id 17592186124789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8cade90-de90-5cc5-a96f-4b62957e1afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id 17592186121332} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1a034d-0611-5f21-a47f-fb41d7d10986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186121335} {:answer/id #uuid "72b6d84b-4599-5ae1-87e9-228fcb3fc5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/id 17592186426052} {:answer/id #uuid "ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}" :db/id 17592186426060} {:answer/id #uuid "c784e28a-945a-50b9-a3c3-88323f6f5bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\mathbb{R}}^{n}" :db/id 17592186426062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}" :db/id 17592186426060} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cf5fde-aae3-5ac2-808d-cd031ad46138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186121331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b33961fd-1191-51c9-841c-0bdd04b0318b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140766" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\{0\\}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186272237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e2978f-44c4-5ccc-a792-625eb51d9546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186269420} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0c5b92-9bb1-5e5e-b9ec-edf4c0497369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{n}\\}" :db/id 17592186270474} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9fdfe6-8dc9-510f-9c18-69a49e29297b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id 17592186270666} {:answer/id #uuid "c3310529-53bb-5e2d-a3be-0786dc03cb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{1}\\}" :db/id 17592186270996} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8902d1-b96f-5746-b901-b960abac5e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186271337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9fdfe6-8dc9-510f-9c18-69a49e29297b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id 17592186270666} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b2b2ef-efa9-59ec-95ce-60b79c07943a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a09c603d-a3ea-5c23-b944-39ca22d1abd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141192" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=\\underline{\\phantom{n}}=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &={\\color{blue}n}=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186125167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec134f4-8ac1-5fd3-a994-79031801df9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186123603} {:answer/id #uuid "e52ece27-d671-527d-aac5-dac055efd06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186123604} {:answer/id #uuid "2e01b189-5d9a-582b-a3da-d616b84586a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186123605} {:answer/id #uuid "32960b14-b04a-59a2-9224-0b8a82626700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186123606} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffd9fe3-d1f5-5a30-b5aa-c6b01c0e6506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186123607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e01b189-5d9a-582b-a3da-d616b84586a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186123605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eda024b-b844-536e-bf1a-f1efd2375bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186123602}] :question/id #uuid "f935b331-7ef7-5abb-b2c6-c78c4160f9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-47586" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $n \\times n$ matrix and $\\det(A) \\ne 0$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $A$ has no inverse\n2. $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$ has the empty set as a basis\n3. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\det(A)\\ne0$, the matrix $A$ is invertible. Thus statement I is false.\n\nThe homogeneous equation $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf0$ has only the trivial solution, so $\\operatorname{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf0\\}$. The empty set is linearly independent and its span is $\\{\\mathbf0\\}$, so it is a basis of this null space. Thus statement II is true.\n\nAn invertible $n\\times n$ matrix has a pivot in every column, so statement III is true. Therefore, the correct answer is II and III only."}]]